ifically what are  the concrete steps that you  think would make this country more equal  well firstly the government could act on  the recommendations of the Lamy review  which looked at disproportionality in  our criminal justice system and those  recommendations 35 of them have been  there since September 2017 secondly the  government could act on the Wendy  Williams lessons-learned review in two  Windrush which was published in March  again you know 30 recommendations there  to be acted upon and one specifically  Sophie with regard to the Windrush  compensation scheme in its first year of  operation it has only compensated 60  people to a total of 360 thousand pounds  now of course I'm pleased for those 60  people but we're talking about thousands  of people who were wronged over  generations that such a small number has  been compensated only that number is a  chronic failure of political leadership  the government needs to focus it needs  to step up to the plate and it needs to  act what one of the things that many  people feel very strongly about who are  out protesting in recent days and weeks  is the police and the way that the  police treat people of different races  and in 2018 to 19 black people in the UK  9 times more likely to be stopped and  searched by police as white people over  three times as likely to be arrested  they were more than 5 times as likely to  have force used against them by police  do you think there is an issue with the  way that police in the UK deal with  people of different races  well the Lamy review identified issues  right through the system that needed  what do you think what do you think I'm  interested do you think that there is a  racism issue in the police actively well  look we've had great progress since the  McPherson report of 1999 which of course  did identify institutional racism there  has been a lot of progress since then  but there are still disproportionate  outcomes you are absolutely right to  identify that Sophie that's what the the  Lamy review did and the government  should look at the 35 recommendations in  that report but having spoken to senior  police officer  having spoken to police officer  representatives which I do on a regular  basis in this role I know that they are  committed to tackling this as well and  ensuring that there is maximum  transparency and ensuring - that the  work that is required on community  confidence in policing is done and I'm  pleased to share that priority with them  I just want to ask again just because I  understand you know your sort desire to  to look at the lambing report to talk  about recommendations but do you feel  there is an issue with the UK police on  the way that they deal with people  different races do you or not well the  the outcomes of our whole criminal  justice system do suggest there are  issues Sophia where I'm reticent around  answering this of course there will be  things like unconscious bias that we of  course have to deal with where you know  I'm not saying individual police  officers aren't doing their best they of  course are but the way to deal with this  is through professionalism and  transparency and my point is it's not  sort of some sort of situation of people  on the one side police on the other the  police officers I speak to are equally  as committed to tackling this as anyone  else and I think working with the police  going forward is the way to solve this  and to make sure that we do have that  community trust in policing that's so  vital  now you very turd using the black lives  matter hashtag er as has kissed armor  and when you go on the official black  lives matter website one of the first  things you see is a link which says  defund the police elsewhere on the  website it says we call for a national  defunding of police do you agree with  those aims well well no I don't want it  to defund the police but what I do say  Sophie is that there is a very deep  anger out there and it's not for me to  start lecturing people as to how to  express that anger here is where I say  again the government has to step up to  act recognise the depth of feeling  recognise the depth of the anger and the  Prime Minister has to show leadership  acknowledge this anger acknowledge the  structural racism inequality that exists  unless I've indicated already do  something about  don't act that's what we need at this  powerful national moment okay now just  to discuss a couple of other things and  shops are gonna be reopening from  tomorrow  many businesses saying that the 2-meter  social distancing rule is making it very  difficult for them to actually open in a  way that means it will be financially  viable and do you think that t meter  rule should be reduced it should be  reduced if the evidence indicated that  it should be and this is where we need  to be so careful Sophie because the  public health position and the health of  the economy go together there's no point  seeing them in conflicts if the  government did ease lockdown too quickly  if it did move to one meter without  there being scientific advice backing  that you would end up with a second peep  which would cause even greater economic  damage so my message to the government  is to be vigilant be cautious approach  this in a structured way and publish the  evidence so we can all see it we can get  that restoration of confidence in the  public health advice which the Dominic  Cummings affair did so much damage to  although really we do know some of the  scientific advice date we I mean we  understand that there is obviously a  risk at one meter  a reduced risk at two meters it's a  political decision isn't it you can't  just always follow the science well it's  a political decision it should be based  on a range of scientific advice Sophie  but if that range of scientific advice  was published then that would restore  confidence in the decisions that the  government is making and it would also  as I say give that transparency that we  require so that we can all judge the  decisions see that they're being making  in an open way everybody can have  confidence in that going forward now the  Sunday Times is reporting that plans to  allow people to change their legal  gender without a medical diagnosis and  self-identify as a different sex have  been scrapped by the government is that  the right decision well what I think we  need to look at in this space is what  trans people actually  think and say to us now I think trans  rights are human rights I've said that  before and I think we need to listen  very carefully going forward in what is  an extremely sensitive area I'm not sure  the government just scrapping plans and  then leaking it out in a newspaper is  the way to deal with this I think we  need a much better way going forward  that sensitive that seeks consensus and  respects everybody's rights now we are  at a time but I do just want to squeeze  and one more question if possible and  we've seen a pretty extraordinary  turnaround in Labour's polling and one  poll this week saying you're just five  points behind the Conservatives the  context the gap was 22 points in  mid-march  Kirstin with the highest ratings of any  opposition leader since tony blair does  that make you think maybe jeremy corbyn  was the problem all along it just makes  it makes me think about the present  Sophie not the past I think what it what  what we're seeing is a vindication here  at the approach that kia stahma is  taking as leader of the Labour Party but  the reality is that at the moment party  politics is not a priority the priority  at the moment is the constructive  opposition and the approach we are  taking with the government to tackle  coronavirus and to attempt to minimize  loss of life that's the priority at the  moment and party politics I think in E's  normal sense can wait for the future  once we're through what is this  extraordinarily challenging global  pandemic very diplomatic  thank you very much having on the  program thank you
 

